{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1544 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "d8812624337292b87de06c996580136376e35e922e899d94fb0fdc4cc08ef4c1" :edb/db-before-t 1543 :edb/db-after-t 1544 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534856 50 #inst "2026-10-08T09:13:22.368Z" 13194139534856 true] [17592186184069 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x)$, defined below?\n${e^{x - 1},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 1, \\\\ 1 - x,\\end{vmatrix}x > 1$\n\n1. $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$\n2. $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$\n3. $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" 13194139534856 false] [17592186184069 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x),$ defined below?\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042135/assets/ba95d95494b67b2da7f5ebeb108fd75a42945291bae5440ff12d2edaa73f2798.png)\n\n- $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$\n\n- $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$\n\n- $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" 13194139534856 true] [17592186184069 1145 "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x = 1$.\n\nLet's start by computing $h(1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh(1) &= e^{1 - 1} \\\\\n&= e^{0} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n$lim_(x → 1^{-})h(x)|= e^{1 - 1}; = e^{0}; = 1; = h(1)$\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n$lim_(x → 1^{+})h(x)|= 1 - 1; = 0; \\ne h(1)$\n\nStatement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=1$, it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous at this point. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534856 false] [17592186184069 1145 "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x = 1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $h(1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(1) & ={e}^{1-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x) & ={e}^{1-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & =h(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x) & =1-1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne h(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStatement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous at this point. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534856 true] [17592186184073 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$, defined below?\n${2 - x,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ 2,\\end{vmatrix}x = 0; \\frac{4}{x + 2}, \\mid x > 0$\n\n1. $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x = 0$\n2. $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x = 0$\n3. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 0$" 13194139534856 false] [17592186184073 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x),$ defined below?\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042135/assets/4985f1b43abee44c927a619c1d8965c98a2283f65d5723429477f7e29dbdc74c.png)\n\n- $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x = 0$\n\n- $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x = 0$\n\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 0$" 13194139534856 true] [17592186184073 1145 "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x = 0$.\n\nFirst, we note that\n\n$$\nf(0) = 2\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x = 0$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n$lim_(x → 0^{-})f(x)|= 2 - 0; = 2$\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x = 0$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n$lim_(x → 0^{+})f(x)|= \\frac{4}{0 + 2}; = \\frac{4}{2}; = 2; = f(0)$\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=0$, which implies that the function is continuous at $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534856 false] [17592186184073 1145 "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x = 0.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x = 0$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =2-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x = 0$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\frac{4}{0+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & =f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=0,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534856 true] [17592186184161 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315245 13194139534856 true] [17592186184161 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315252 13194139534856 true] [17592186184161 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315287 13194139534856 true] [17592186184161 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315294 13194139534856 true] [17592186184161 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315301 13194139534856 true] [17592186184165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315238 13194139534856 true] [17592186184165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315259 13194139534856 true] [17592186184165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315266 13194139534856 true] [17592186184165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315273 13194139534856 true] [17592186184165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315280 13194139534856 true] [17592186315238 1017 #edb/ref 17592186315239 13194139534856 true] [17592186315238 1089 "q-104384" 13194139534856 true] [17592186315238 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534856 true] [17592186315238 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3},\\,\\, & x<2 \\\\ 8,\\,\\, & x=2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2},\\, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" 13194139534856 true] [17592186315238 1113 #uuid "c4959073-98d3-5dc2-bb77-936c253ada61" 13194139534856 true] [17592186315238 1145 "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x=2.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=2$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & =f(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=2$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & =2(2)^{2} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & =f(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=2,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534856 true] [17592186315239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315240 13194139534856 true] [17592186315239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315241 13194139534856 true] [17592186315239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315242 13194139534856 true] [17592186315239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315243 131